\qc\b\fs36 AWS EKS .NET 8 Deployment Guide\b0\fs24\par
\qc Infrastructure, container build, Kubernetes deployment, verification, and troubleshooting\par
\ql\par
\b 1. Project Overview\b0\par
This project provisions AWS infrastructure with Terraform and deploys a .NET 8 Web API to Amazon EKS. The application is packaged as a Docker image, stored in Amazon ECR, and deployed to private EKS worker nodes. An internal AWS Load Balancer exposes the application only to clients with VPC connectivity.\par
\par
The completed flow is:\par
\bullet  Terraform creates the VPC, subnets, routing, NAT Gateway, ECR, bastion, and EKS cluster.\par
\bullet  Docker builds the .NET 8 application image.\par
\bullet  ECR stores the image.\par
\bullet  Kubernetes creates two application replicas.\par
\bullet  An internal LoadBalancer Service exposes the application on port 80 and forwards traffic to container port 8080.\par
\bullet  SSM port forwarding allows a developer outside the VPC to open Swagger locally.\par
\par
\b 2. AWS Architecture\b0\par
\f1 Developer laptop\par
    | AWS CLI, Terraform, Docker, and SSM\par
    v\par
\f0 Private bastion EC2 instance\par
    | private EKS API access\par
    v\par
Private EKS cluster\par
    |\par
    +-- two t3.micro worker nodes\par
            |\par
            +-- two dotnet-app pods\par
\par
The application Service creates an internal AWS Load Balancer. The Load Balancer is not public and cannot be opened directly from a normal home network.\par
\par
\b 3. Important Project Locations\b0\par
Terraform root: /home/icanio-10009/Downloads/EKS task\par
.NET application: /home/icanio-10009/Downloads/Sample-Application/dotnet\par
Terraform entry point: main.tf\par
Kubernetes deployment: k8s/deployment.yaml\par
Kubernetes Service: k8s/service.yaml\par
Dockerfile: /home/icanio-10009/Downloads/Sample-Application/dotnet/Dockerfile\par
\par
\b 4. Terraform State Backend\b0\par
Terraform state is stored remotely in Amazon S3. The configured state bucket is:\par
\f1 dotnet-app-terraform-state-123806349463\par
\f0
State key:\par
\f1 dotnet-app/dev/terraform.tfstate\par
\f0
S3 native lockfiles are enabled with use_lockfile = true. This prevents two Terraform processes from changing the same state simultaneously. The bucket should have versioning, encryption, and public access blocking enabled.\par
\par
Initialize the backend with:\par
\f1 terraform init -migrate-state -backend-config=backend.hcl\par
\f0\par
Never commit account-specific backend credentials or replace the backend bucket with an example placeholder.\par
\par
\b 5. Networking Resources\b0\par
\b VPC\b0\par
CIDR: 10.0.0.0/16. The VPC contains all private and public AWS resources.\par
\par
\b Public subnets\b0\par
10.0.0.0/24 in ap-south-1a\par
10.0.1.0/24 in ap-south-1b\par
\par
Public subnets host the Internet Gateway route and NAT Gateway.\par
\par
\b Private subnets\b0\par
10.0.10.0/24 in ap-south-1a\par
10.0.11.0/24 in ap-south-1b\par
\par
Private subnets host EKS worker nodes and the bastion. They do not receive public IP addresses.\par
\par
\b Internet Gateway\b0\par
The Internet Gateway provides internet routing for public subnets.\par
\par
\b NAT Gateway\b0\par
The NAT Gateway lets private resources make outbound connections without accepting inbound internet traffic. It is required for worker nodes to reach AWS services and download required resources. NAT Gateways are billable, so a single NAT Gateway is used for development cost control.\par
\par
\b Elastic IP\b0\par
The NAT Gateway uses one Elastic IP address.\par
\par
\b 6. ECR Repository\b0\par
The application image is stored at:\par
\f1 123806349463.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/dotnet-app-dev/dotnet-app\par
\f0
ECR is used because EKS worker nodes need a private container registry. Worker nodes have AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryReadOnly permission. The repository scans images on push and keeps the latest ten images through a lifecycle policy.\par
\par
\b 7. EKS Cluster\b0\par
Cluster name: dotnet-app-dev-eks\par
Region: ap-south-1\par
Kubernetes version: 1.33\par
Endpoint: private only\par
\par
The EKS API has private access enabled and public access disabled. This reduces exposure but means kubectl must run from the bastion, a VPN-connected client, Direct Connect, or another VPC-connected host.\par
\par
\b 8. IAM Resources\b0\par
The project creates:\par
\bullet  EKS cluster IAM role with AmazonEKSClusterPolicy.\par
\bullet  Worker node IAM role with EKS worker, CNI, ECR read-only, and SSM policies.\par
\bullet  Bastion IAM role with SSM management and EKS describe permissions.\par
\bullet  Bastion instance profile.\par
\bullet  IAM OIDC provider for the EKS cluster.\par
\bullet  EKS access entry for the bastion.\par
\bullet  AmazonEKSClusterAdminPolicy association for the bastion.\par
\par
For production, cluster-admin access should be replaced with least-privilege permissions.\par
\par
\b 9. Bastion Host\b0\par
The bastion is an SSM-managed EC2 instance in a private subnet. SSH and inbound security group rules are not required. SSM is used for secure administration.\par
\par
Bastion instance ID: i-06cb07072fd8099e3\par
\par
Connect with:\par
\f1 aws ssm start-session --target i-06cb07072fd8099e3 --region ap-south-1\par
\f0\par
The bastion is also used to run kubectl because the EKS API endpoint is private.\par
\par
\b 10. EKS Worker Nodes and Add-ons\b0\par
Node group: dotnet-app-dev-eks-default\par
Instance type: t3.micro\par
Desired nodes: 2\par
Minimum nodes: 1\par
Maximum nodes: 3\par
\par
The node group uses Amazon Linux 2023 x86_64 images and runs in private subnets. The default was changed from t3.medium because the account rejected t3.medium as not Free Tier eligible. Free Tier eligibility varies by account and region.\par
\par
Managed add-ons:\par
\bullet  CoreDNS: DNS resolution inside Kubernetes.\par
\bullet  kube-proxy: Kubernetes Service networking on nodes.\par
\bullet  VPC CNI: AWS VPC networking for pods.\par
\par
\b 11. .NET 8 Application\b0\par
The application project is dotnet.csproj. Its target framework is net8.0 and its published assembly is dotnet.dll. The Dockerfile uses the .NET 8 SDK and ASP.NET runtime images. The application listens on port 8080.\par
\par
The application was verified with:\par
\f1 dotnet restore\par
dotnet build --configuration Release\par
\f0\par
The build output is:\par
\f1 bin/Release/net8.0/dotnet.dll\par
\f0\par
\b 12. Build and Push the Image\b0\par
From the application directory:\par
\f1 cd /home/icanio-10009/Downloads/Sample-Application/dotnet\par
\f0\par
Authenticate to ECR:\par
\f1 aws ecr get-login-password --region ap-south-1 | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin 123806349463.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com\par
\f0\par
Build the image:\par
\f1 docker build -t 123806349463.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/dotnet-app-dev/dotnet-app:v1 .\par
\f0\par
Push the image:\par
\f1 docker push 123806349463.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/dotnet-app-dev/dotnet-app:v1\par
\f0\par
The image used by the deployment was successfully pushed with tag v1.\par
\par
\b 13. Kubernetes Deployment\b0\par
The Deployment creates two replicas. Two replicas provide basic availability and allow traffic to continue if one pod is restarted.\par
\par
The container exposes port 8080 and sets ASPNETCORE_URLS to http://+:8080. Readiness and liveness probes use TCP port 8080. CPU and memory requests reserve capacity, while limits prevent unlimited resource consumption.\par
\par
Apply the manifests from a VPC-connected host:\par
\f1 kubectl apply -f k8s/deployment.yaml -f k8s/service.yaml\par
kubectl rollout status deployment/dotnet-app\par
kubectl get pods -l app=dotnet-app\par
kubectl get svc dotnet-app\par
\f0\par
\b 14. Kubernetes Service\b0\par
The Service is type LoadBalancer and has an internal AWS Load Balancer annotation. Port 80 on the Load Balancer forwards to port 8080 in the pods. The Load Balancer is intentionally private.\par
\par
Current internal DNS name:\par
\f1 internal-a490e07e72cc24c6ca6b258a65a7757c-1099363377.ap-south-1.elb.amazonaws.com\par
\f0\par
\b 15. Access Swagger from a Laptop\b0\par
A browser outside the VPC cannot directly access the internal Load Balancer. Use SSM port forwarding through the bastion. Install the Session Manager plugin and keep the tunnel terminal open.\par
\par
\f1 PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH" aws ssm start-session --target i-06cb07072fd8099e3 --document-name AWS-StartPortForwardingSessionToRemoteHost --parameters '{"host":["internal-a490e07e72cc24c6ca6b258a65a7757c-1099363377.ap-south-1.elb.amazonaws.com"],"portNumber":["80"],"localPortNumber":["8080"]}' --region ap-south-1\par
\f0\par
Then open:\par
\f1 http://localhost:8080/swagger\par
\f0\par
The tunnel was verified with HTTP status 200. If localhost refuses the connection, the SSM tunnel is not running or the Session Manager plugin is missing.\par
\par
\b 16. Verification Checklist\b0\par
Terraform validation:\par
\f1 terraform fmt -check -recursive\par
terraform validate\par
terraform plan\par
\f0\par
Expected final plan result:\par
\f1 No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.\par
\f0\par
Check the cluster:\par
\f1 aws eks describe-cluster --name dotnet-app-dev-eks --region ap-south-1\par
\f0\par
Expected status: ACTIVE.\par
\par
Check worker nodes:\par
\f1 aws eks describe-nodegroup --cluster-name dotnet-app-dev-eks --nodegroup-name dotnet-app-dev-eks-default --region ap-south-1\par
\f0\par
Expected status: ACTIVE.\par
\par
Check application pods:\par
\f1 kubectl get pods -l app=dotnet-app\par
\f0\par
Expected result: two pods with READY 1/1 and STATUS Running.\par
\par
Check Swagger:\par
\f1 curl -I http://localhost:8080/swagger/index.html\par
\f0\par
Expected result: HTTP 200.\par
\par
\b 17. Why Eight Pods May Appear\b0\par
The application creates two pods. Other pods belong to Kubernetes system components:\par
\bullet  2 dotnet-app pods.\par
\bullet  2 CoreDNS pods.\par
\bullet  2 VPC CNI pods.\par
\bullet  2 kube-proxy pods.\par
\par
To view only application pods:\par
\f1 kubectl get pods -l app=dotnet-app\par
\f0\par
\b 18. Troubleshooting Summary\b0\par
\b Expired AWS credentials\b0\par
Clear old environment variables and refresh the AWS login:\par
\f1 unset AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY AWS_SESSION_TOKEN AWS_SECURITY_TOKEN\naws login\par
\f0\par
\b Stale Terraform lock\b0\par
Only force-unlock after confirming no Terraform process is running. Use the lock ID shown in the Terraform error.\par
\par
\b Node group CREATE_FAILED\b0\par
Check the node group health details. The original t3.medium setting was rejected by the account's Free Tier restriction. The current development default is t3.micro.\par
\par
\b kubectl downloads an XML error\b0\par
Use an explicit Kubernetes client version matching the cluster:\par
\f1 curl -fL https://dl.k8s.io/release/v1.33.0/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl -o /usr/local/bin/kubectl\par
chmod 0755 /usr/local/bin/kubectl\par
\f0\par
\b kubectl connects to localhost:8080\b0\par
Set the kubeconfig explicitly:\par
\f1 export KUBECONFIG=/root/.kube/config\par
\f0\par
\b Browser cannot reach internal Load Balancer\b0\par
Use SSM port forwarding and browse to localhost:8080. Do not make the Load Balancer public unless the security requirements explicitly allow it.\par
\par
\b 19. Costs and Cleanup\b0\par
Potentially billable resources include EKS, NAT Gateway, Elastic IP, EC2 worker nodes, bastion EC2, internal Load Balancer, ECR storage and scans, CloudWatch logs, and data transfer. Development resources should be destroyed when no longer needed.\par
\par
Delete the Kubernetes workload:\par
\f1 kubectl delete -f k8s/deployment.yaml -f k8s/service.yaml\par
\f0\par
Destroy the Terraform infrastructure only after confirming the correct state and environment:\par
\f1 terraform destroy\par
\f0\par
Never run terraform destroy against shared or production state without review.\par
\par
\b 20. Final Result\b0\par
The completed environment contains a private EKS cluster, two healthy t3.micro worker nodes, a private ECR repository, an SSM-managed bastion, an internal Load Balancer, and a running .NET 8 application. The application deployment was verified with two Running pods and a successful HTTP 200 response from Swagger through the SSM tunnel.\par
